WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.040
Imagine you are hungry and all you have at home

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.200
is a coconut, some rice and an egg.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.500
If you no clue and zero creative thoughts

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.300
you’d probably eat the raw egg with unboiled

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.540
hard rice while staring at the coconut.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.100
If however, you have memories of your dad

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.520
cracking the coconut, and your mom boiling the rice,

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.160
your brain can connect the dots
and form a new creative idea.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.480
You boil the rice inside coconut water,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.560
then fry it with the egg and add coconut flakes.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.500
Voila!

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.600
Enjoy the your meal!"

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.340
Creativity is our ability to look at a problem

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.480
and come up with a good solution to solve it.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.640
Once we understand this, we realize

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.400
that it has nothing to do with the subject matter,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.580
job or what we study.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.700
There are creative inventors and creative artists,

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.680
but there are also very creative

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.620
cleaners and highly creative teachers.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.200
People we worship for their creativity,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.180
often just connect different ideas in a beautiful new way

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.160
and make them commercially successful.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.080
While all of us are creative,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.680
we differ in the way that we are and to which extend.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.700
Michael Kirton
came up with the Adaption-Innovation Theory.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.880
He believes that when we solve problems

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.020
we are either more adaptive or more innovative.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.400
People who are more adaptive-creative,
try to do things better.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.680
People who are more innovative-creative,
try to do things differently.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.380
To solve a specific problem,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.500
say that of smelly cat litter,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.860
both types would use a different approach.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.040
More adaptive types look for a solution inside the box.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.260
They might try to create better cat litter

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.400
by looking it's chemical properties,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.780
then increase the size of the sand particles

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.360
and finally add some refreshing tropical scent.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.119
More innovative creatives, think outside the litter box.

00:01:57.119 --> 00:01:59.260
They come up with cat diapers,

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.420
cat schools for good manners or a robocat.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.060
But there are also other differences.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:13.820
Psychologist J.P. Guilford and some others
argue that there is divergent and convergent thinking.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.520
More divergent thinkers are better at coming up

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.420
with many ideas when they see a problem.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.840
Convergent thinkers see all the details

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.740
and are better at narrowing down the options.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.400
A diverse team is hence usually most effective

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.080
when trying to solve problems.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.420
A divergent thinker can list many ideas.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.220
After a convergent thinker can then look at each

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.660
option in detail and then pick the best one.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:43.800
The result is better than if anyone would
do it by themselves.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.860
A murder mystery experiment

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.940
involving two groups of students

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.840
shows how creative diversity works.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.740
Group A was full of students from the same
background.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.820
Group B were also all similar but joined by
one single stranger.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.820
The students from group A enjoyed the process

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.680
and felt like they worked together every well.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.760
The students in group B didn’t like having
a stranger in their team,

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.100
but they solved the mystery twice as fast

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.840
and won the race.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.320
The researchers concluded

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.100
that the stranger added a new perspective,

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.940
making the group think harder,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.460
and making them more careful of drawing
fast conclusions

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.700
or falling into group think.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.120
The result was a more intelligent
problem-solving process.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.720
If we want to become more creative,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.460
we first have to build a collection of knowledge and memories,

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.380
ideally by seeking new experiences.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.760
Only then we can increase the dots in our

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.880
brain that we can connect.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.960
Clayton Christensen from Harvard Business School,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.720
recommends that parents should fix things at home

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.280
all by themselves.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.300
Their children then learn that problems can be solved

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.200
by ourselves and in many different ways.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.800
Jack Matson,
professor of creativity at Penn State University,

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.420
recommends to dress for failure.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.280
This gives us a new perspective

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.040
and the ability to play new roles.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.880
Marc Schwyn, founder of MinuteVideos

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.720
suggests practising saying yes!

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.440
Because whenever we say yes,

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.360
we open the door to a new experiences.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.820
Can you help me?

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.640
Want to try my ice-cream?

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.120
Can i talk to you?

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:28.300
Yes! Yes! Yes!

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.540
We recommend you to do one thing
you have never done before

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.460
every day for at least one week, maybe a month.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.040
Call your weird aunt,

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.600
talk to a stranger,

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.440
eat using your left hand,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.380
or take a really really cold shower.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.720
Every evening write down what you did

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.520
and what you’ve learned from it.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.180
Start with the first new thing right now

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.220
and share your experience in the comments below
right after.

